---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { SEEKING } from "../data/instruments.js";
import { PATHS, DOOR_WORD } from "../data/paths.js";

// Contribution is routed by what someone already has, not by what they call themselves.
// A quantitative economist is a researcher and a data scientist both; asking them to pick
// an identity sorts people into boxes none of them fit, and it contradicts CRediT, which
// exists precisely to credit contributions rather than titles.
//
// Each path carries its real capacity. One person cannot service five doors, and a door
// advertised as open that answers in three weeks costs more trust than one marked honestly.
// PATHS lives in src/data/paths.js so this page and the homepage cannot disagree.
---

<Page
  title="How to contribute"
  description="Six ways in, routed by what you already have rather than by job title: a published paper, a subject you know, a question and data, skills and time, an instrument of your own, or simulation output nobody can open. Four are open now; each carries its real capacity."
  current="/contribute/"
>
  <h1>How to contribute</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Routed by what you already have, not by what you do for a living — a quantitative
    economist is a researcher and a data scientist both, and picking one would be a
    fiction. Each path below carries its honest capacity, because one person cannot
    service {DOOR_WORD[PATHS.length] ?? PATHS.length} doors at once.
  </p>

  <section class="sec" id="ways">
    <div class="rail">Ways in<span>find your row</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you already have</th><th>The path</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {PATHS.map((p) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{p.have}</td>
                <td><a href={`#${p.anchor}`}>{p.path}</a></td>
                <td>
                  <span class={p.status === "open" ? "chip open" : "chip later"}>{p.status}</span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        <b>Case by case</b> is not a soft no — it is real and it has happened. It means
        capacity is the constraint, so ask before investing time rather than after.
      </p>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>There is no row here for fieldwork-based research, and that is a closed door
          rather than an oversight.</b> Interviews, transcripts, household rosters,
          participatory maps and life histories cannot be accepted: this site publishes
          aggregate data only, has no confidentiality infrastructure and no ethics route,
          and its visualization standard requires drill-down to the unit of analysis — which
          for that work would itself be a disclosure harm.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">
          Six doors are advertised as "routed by what you already have" while all six are
          shut to an entire class of research, and that exclusion used to be left to be
          inferred from the limits page. Stated here instead. What remains open to that work is
          <a href="#attack">breaking an evaluation</a> — which needs no data at all, and is
          where a framing critique would land hardest.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="published">
    <div class="rail">Path one<span>a published paper</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>The research is already refereed. Now make it usable.</h2>
      <p>
        A paper's analysis is its most reusable part and its least accessible: the figures
        are flattened into a PDF, and the question a reader wants to ask — <em>what happens
        if I change this assumption?</em> — cannot be asked. That is what gets built here,
        on top of work that has already passed review.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the cleanest path on the site, because the objections that apply to the
        others do not apply to it:
      </p>
      <div class="sides">
        <div class="side good">
          <h3>Why it is clean</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><b>No preprint problem.</b> Your paper is published. Nothing here affects a
              submission you have already made</li>
            <li><b>No peer-review caveat.</b> The research is refereed; the instrument is
              an addition to it, not a substitute</li>
            <li><b>Authorship is settled.</b> No negotiation — the instrument is a separate
              artifact that cites your paper</li>
            <li><b>It is fast.</b> The hard interpretive work is done, so there is no
              waiting on anybody</li>
            <li><b>You get something to present.</b> A conference question from the floor
              — <em>what if you change that assumption?</em> — becomes something you answer
              live rather than defer</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>The order matters, and it changed after a reader found the trap.</b> The
          replication is done <b>from the paper alone, before any data is asked for</b> —
          it needs neither your data nor your code, and a worked case proved it. Your data
          is only needed for the dashboard. That way, if the rebuild contradicts your
          published result, you have not handed over the material that produced the
          contradiction: the finding rests on your published text, as any replication does,
          and the conversation about data happens afterwards with that already known.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">
          Previously this path asked for data first. But "authorship is settled, no
          negotiation" holds only while the rebuild agrees, and handing data over first
          means commissioning an adversarial re-analysis of your own best work. So the
          sequence is reversed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sides">
        <div class="side warn">
          <h3>Two things that stop it dead</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><b>The data.</b> If you cannot share the data behind the paper, there is no
              instrument. This is the first question asked, and it ends more of these
              conversations than anything else</li>
            <li><b>Publisher copyright.</b> Findings and data are yours to re-express, but
              the journal usually holds the typeset figures and text. Everything is rebuilt
              from the data; the published figures are never reproduced</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The most immediate use is usually the least academic one: <a href="/#present">having
        something to stand in front of</a> at a conference, a defence or a grant panel.
        Nothing about your record changes; what changes is what you can show.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>It is not a figure service.</b> What gets added is not a prettier chart: the
          pipeline is re-runnable from source, the models are scored against whatever
          baselines the paper reports and against a naive rival if it does not name one, and
          the gates are published including any that fail. If re-running your analysis honestly weakens a result, the instrument says
          so — which is the same deal everyone else here gets.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="interpret">
    <div class="rail">Path two<span>a subject you know</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>{SEEKING.length} instruments are built and waiting for a conclusion</h2>
      <p>
        No data to find, no code to write, nothing to build. The measurement is finished on
        all of them; what is missing is what the result <em>means</em> in its own field, and
        each instrument states its own specific gap rather than a generic invitation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Write that interpretation and you are <b>first author</b>. You are also accountable
        for it, which is the whole point — the reason the pages currently stop short of a
        conclusion is that nobody involved is qualified to draw one.
      </p>
      <h3>How much work it actually is</h3>
      <p>
        A domain expert cannot decide whether to say yes to "write the conclusion" without
        knowing the size of the ask, and the page used to leave that blank. So, concretely:
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>How long is the writing?</b></td>
              <td>Roughly 800–1,500 words: what the result means in your field, which of
                the instrument's assumptions you accept, and what you would need before
                believing it. Nobody has done one yet, so treat this as the intended shape
                rather than a demonstrated one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>By when?</b></td>
              <td>There is no deadline. If it takes six months the page stays exactly as
                it is, marked as still seeking an author. We will not chase you.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Do you have to read the code?</b></td>
              <td>No. Every instrument has a plain-language walkthrough and a glossary at
                the top of its article. If a term stopped you, that is a defect in the
                page and worth telling us about on its own.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Can you say "do not publish this"?</b></td>
              <td><b>Yes, and that ends it.</b> If your reading is that the instrument
                should not carry a conclusion — because the method cannot support one, or
                because publishing it would do harm — we take that as the answer and the
                page says so. You do not owe us a usable conclusion in exchange for
                looking.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Can you withdraw after seeing the draft?</b></td>
              <td>Yes, at any point before it goes live, with no explanation required.
                Nothing is published under your name that you have not read in place.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>What if you disagree with the instrument?</b></td>
              <td>Then the published conclusion says you disagree, and why. A conclusion
                that contradicts the measurement is a better outcome than no conclusion,
                and it is not a failed collaboration.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>The risk is not symmetrical, and it is yours</h3>
      <p>
        Several of these instruments touch official statistics, ministry programmes, or
        provincial figures that somebody defends professionally. If you are a doctoral
        candidate, or you work anywhere near the agency whose numbers an instrument
        recalculates, putting your name to a conclusion carries a cost we do not carry.
        Naming that plainly is more useful than pretending it away, so:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>You choose the framing.</b> Where a finding can be stated as a question
          about method rather than a verdict about an institution, and both are honest,
          the choice is yours.</li>
        <li><b>You are never the only name.</b> The build is credited separately under
          <a href="#credit">CRediT roles</a>, so a reader can see which parts are yours.</li>
        <li><b>Nothing goes out under your affiliation</b> unless you write it in
          yourself.</li>
        <li><b>You can write it and ask us to hold it</b> until a thesis is defended or a
          contract ends. We will hold it.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <a href="/#instruments">See the {SEEKING.length} openings</a>, each with what it
        found and what it is missing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="new">
    <div class="rail">Path three<span>a question and data</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Starting from a question, with gates before anything is built</h2>
      <p>
        The most valuable path and the easiest to abandon halfway, so it is deliberately
        gated. Nothing gets built until the first three steps are done in writing.
      </p>

      <h3>What happens, in order</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><b>You say what the question is</b> and point at the data. A conversation, not a form.</li>
        <li><b>I scope it</b> — what can be built from data you can share, what cannot, and where the honest limits are. If it cannot be built well, you are told then, not later.</li>
        <li><b>Thresholds are agreed before anything runs.</b> What would count as the model working, and what would count as it failing. Written down first.</li>
        <li><b>The instrument gets built.</b> You see it while it is being built, not at the end.</li>
        <li><b>You write the interpretation.</b> What the result means, what surprised you, what it does not settle.</li>
        <li><b>It publishes</b> with your name as lead author, and a DOI if you want one.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>Step 3 is the one that matters.</b> Thresholds are never moved to fit an
          outcome. If the instrument does not support your finding, the page says so, with
          the numbers. That is not a threat — it is the reason a reader should believe the
          cases where it does.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        <b>Data you can lawfully share, from a source with a stated licence</b> — aggregate
        only, no human subjects. Note the wording: not "open data". Several sources this
        site already depends on need a free account and an API key — NASA Earthdata,
        Copernicus, BPS, UN Comtrade — and one, EOG's night-lights product, went
        subscriber-only in 2026. Calling that "open" would have been a claim the
        <a href="/standards/#sources">sources page</a> contradicts. What actually matters is
        whether you may share it and whether its terms are stated. Not a judgement about
        your work — a small operation cannot carry ethics review or personal data, and the
        <a href="#limits">limits</a> below say so plainly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="attack">
    <div class="rail">Path four<span>skills, no subject</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Replicate or break something that already exists</h2>
      <p>
        Per hour spent this is the most valuable contribution anyone can make here, and it
        is the only path that produces <b>evidence rather than inventory</b>. Every other
        route adds an instrument; this one establishes whether the existing ones survive
        scrutiny. An instrument that a named engineer attacked and failed to break is worth
        more than three nobody checked.
      </p>
      <p>
        It needs no subject, no data and no permission. Pick an instrument, read its gates,
        and try to make them fail. <b>The code is public</b> —
        <a href="https://github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy">github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy</a>, MIT — so the pipeline,
        the thresholds and the gate records are all readable and re-runnable. This path was undoable while the repository was
        private. It was, and it is not now.
      </p>

      <h3>Break the evaluation — a day</h3>
      <p>
        Is the metric right for the problem? Did a baseline see the test set? Does the
        cross-validation actually prevent leakage? <b>If you succeed, that gets published</b>
        — with Validation credit.
      </p>

      <h3>Beat the baseline — a week</h3>
      <p>
        Every model here is scored against a naive rival that already contains the easy
        knowledge. Build a better rival. If yours wins, the case is weakened and that is
        published; if it loses, the case is stronger. Either outcome is worth having.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>Students: this is your path.</b> It needs no data, no subject and nobody's
          permission, and the work is judged exactly as everyone else's is — which is what
          makes the credit worth having. Breaking an evaluation that a published instrument
          rests on is a stronger portfolio piece than another tutorial project.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">
          What this site cannot do at the moment is <b>supervise a thesis</b>. One person
          reviews everything here, and carrying a project through to a defence is a
          different job from the one being offered. <a href="#new">Path three</a> stays open
          to anyone — student or not — who has a question and data they are genuinely able
          to share.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><b>Three specific openings right now:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>transit-equity</b> has two failing hard gates — timetable sanity and
            network integrity. Are they fatal or cosmetic?</li>
          <li><b>rice-security</b>'s R² goes from −11.09 to 0.82 through an OLS
            calibration. Is that legitimate, or is it fitting the answer?</li>
          <li><b>fire-haze</b> scores a 3.8% event class with ROC-AUC. Is precision-recall
            the honest metric instead?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="bring">
    <div class="rail">Case by case<span>your own instrument</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Bringing an instrument you built yourself</h2>
      <p>
        This works — every instrument here was built before it had a domain author. But the
        constraint is worth stating plainly: there are already
        <b>{SEEKING.length} instruments without one</b>. The shortage is domain authors, not
        instruments, and another unpartnered build makes that worse rather than better.
      </p>
      <p>
        So it is welcome with <b>a named target</b> — a person or an institution you have
        already approached about interpreting it. Bring that and the conversation is short.
        Without it, the honest answer is that it would join a queue that is not moving.
      </p>
      <p class="dim">
        It also has to meet the <a href="/standards/">published standard</a>, which includes
        the visualization criteria — the part most often skipped.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="simulation">
    <div class="rail">Case by case<span>simulation output</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Output nobody outside your group can open</h2>
      <p>
        A molecular-dynamics trajectory, a CFD field, a finite-element run, a model
        ensemble. The science is finished — the simulation <em>is</em> the analysis — but the
        result lives in a file format that a referee, a co-author abroad or a funder cannot
        open, and the desktop tools that can open it were never built for sharing.
      </p>
      <p>
        What gets built is a <b>browser-native, interrogable version</b>: scrub the
        trajectory at the frame rate it was written, pick a residue or a cell and watch every
        other panel filter to it, change the cutoff or the smoothing window and see the
        answer move.
      </p>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>This one has no gates, and the page will say so.</b> Every instrument here is
          scored against a naive rival that already contains the easy knowledge. A
          visualization of your simulation has nothing to beat and nothing to fail — there is
          no estimate being made. So it is delivered as the <a href="/standards/#visualization">
          visualization stage alone</a>, judged against those six criteria, and it makes
          <b>no validation claim whatsoever</b>. Calling it an instrument would empty the word.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>Check the existing tools first — they are probably better.</b> MDAnalysis and
          MDTraj read XTC, DCD, TRR, NetCDF and PDB. Mol*, NGL and MDsrv already stream
          trajectories in a browser; ParaView Glance, VTK.js and Trame do CFD and FEM;
          neuroglancer streams volumes far larger than yours. If one of those solves it, use
          it. A bespoke rebuild of a solved problem is a queue for something you can
          already install.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">
          What is left after that is narrower and real: a viewer built around <em>your
          specific finding</em> rather than the general format — the two quantities that
          matter next to each other, the frame where the argument happens, and a page a
          ministry counterpart can open without installing anything.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Three things that decide whether it is feasible</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Size.</b> A modest trajectory is gigabytes. The web version is a derived
          representation — subsampled frames, precomputed contact maps, quantised
          coordinates — and agreeing what may be thrown away is the first conversation.</li>
        <li><b>Format.</b> XTC, DCD, TRR, NetCDF, PDB and whatever your analysis scripts
          emit. Ask before assuming; this is the part that most often makes it a no.</li>
        <li><b>What to show is your knowledge, not mine.</b> A rotating molecule is
          worthless. Which quantity answers the question — RMSF, occupancy, a contact map,
          a pocket volume — is a domain judgement, and it has to come from you.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="dim">
        <b>Case by case</b> because these are deep formats and capacity is the constraint.
        A short description of the output and the question it answers is enough to find out.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="credit">
    <div class="rail">Credit<span>CRediT, not authorship</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Naming what each person did</h2>
      <p>
        The Contributor Roles Taxonomy replaces "author or not" with fourteen named roles.
        It is a published standard, used by many journals, and it is the honest way to
        describe this arrangement — and the reason paths are routed by contribution rather
        than by job title.
      </p>
      <h3>The deal is not the same on every path</h3>
      <p>
        Credit follows what was actually done, so a path that adds an instrument to
        finished research cannot carry the same credit as one where the analysis is
        designed here. Stated per path so nobody has to infer it:
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>First author</th><th>What the build takes</th><th>Joint publication?</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="#published">Published paper → instrument</a></td>
              <td>Already settled — your paper</td>
              <td>Software, Visualization, Data curation <b>on the instrument</b>; your paper
                is cited, not co-authored</td>
              <td class="no">No</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>…and the rebuild produces a finding</b></td>
              <td>Whoever wrote the finding</td>
              <td>+ Formal analysis, Validation — a re-analysis that recovers or contradicts
                something is new work, not rendering</td>
              <td class="yes">Yes, a short separate output</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="#interpret">Interpret one of the ten</a></td>
              <td class="yes">You</td>
              <td>Six roles → <b>methods co-author</b></td>
              <td class="yes">Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="#new">New study, gated</a></td>
              <td class="yes">You</td>
              <td>Six roles → <b>methods co-author</b></td>
              <td class="yes">Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="#attack">Replicate or break</a></td>
              <td>You, on your own finding</td>
              <td>Nothing — it is your result</td>
              <td>Published as its own note</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="#bring">Your instrument</a> · <a href="#simulation">simulation</a></td>
              <td>Agreed before anything starts</td>
              <td>Agreed before anything starts</td>
              <td>Case by case</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>The second row is the one to read twice.</b> A rebuild that fails to reproduce
          something, or recovers a parameter the original no longer has, is a finding — and
          then it publishes as a finding, with you offered co-authorship and a right of
          reply. It does not become joint work automatically, because you may disagree with
          it, and a replication nobody can dissent from is worth nothing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>The six roles, where they apply</h3>
      <p class="dim">
        Rows two and three above — the paths where the analysis is designed here.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Domain author</th><th>Measurement</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Conceptualization</td><td class="yes">yes</td><td class="dim">—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Investigation</td><td class="yes">yes</td><td class="dim">—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Writing – original draft</td><td class="yes">yes</td><td class="dim">—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Resources <span class="dim">— domain data, access</span></td>
              <td class="yes">yes</td><td class="dim">—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Methodology</td><td>shared</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Formal analysis</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Validation</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Software</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Data curation</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visualization</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="yes">yes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Six roles including Methodology and Formal analysis is, under the criteria most
        journals apply, a <b>methods co-authorship</b> — designing the analysis and
        evaluating the models is not a technical service. What it does not claim is
        the finding: you lead, you own the interpretation, and you are accountable for what
        the result means.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p><b>Author order, where there is a joint publication at all.</b> Whoever writes
        the interpretation is first author. Whoever built the measurement is second, as
        methods co-author. On a published-paper build there is no joint publication and
        nothing to order — the paper is cited.</p>
        <p class="dim">
          Stated here so it is never a negotiation later. If a case has several
          contributors, order follows contribution and is agreed before publication — never
          after, and never by seniority.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Where the site is merely run rather than contributed to, that is an <b>editor</b>
        credit and not authorship — the same rule applied to the person who runs it.
      </p>

      <h3>AI assistance, disclosed</h3>
      <p>
        Pipeline and model implementation are produced with AI assistance, under my
        direction. It is stated on every instrument, in the methods, in a
        sentence — because a reader who works it out for themselves is entitled to feel
        misled, and a reader who is told is not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="limits">
    <div class="rail">Limits<span>what is not accepted</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>What this site will not publish</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Personal data.</b> Aggregate only, no exceptions.</li>
        <li><b>Redistributed source data.</b> Pipelines fetch from the origin and cite the
          licence; the instrument publishes derived results, not somebody else's dataset.</li>
        <li><b>Accusations against named companies or projects.</b> Methods and aggregate
          distributions, yes. Naming an actor as culpable, no. <b>This is a liability limit,
          not an ethical position</b>, and a reader was right to say it should not sit here
          unlabelled beside personal data. One person with no institution behind them cannot
          carry a defamation risk in Indonesia. It is a limit on what this site can host, and
          it is a real cost: a case can compute a concession overlap and then be forbidden
          the sentence the overlap exists to support.</li>
        <li><b>A finding without a way to check it.</b> If the gates cannot be stated, the
          instrument cannot be built.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="dim">
        These are limits on the site, not judgements about your work. Some of them exist
        because a small operation cannot carry the risk; the rest are on the
        <a href="/standards/">standards page</a> with the reasoning.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

